# FEATURE: "Did You Mean?" — Self-Prompting Suggestion

## Summary
Add a "Did you mean?" self-prompting feature to the deeds-web app. When users enter queries or seek actions that are ambiguous, misspelled, or likely to fail, the app will suggest corrected queries or alternative prompts they can accept with one click.

## Motivation
- Reduce friction from typos, vague queries, and misconceived commands.
- Improve task completion rates and perceived responsiveness.
- Decrease support requests and repeated failed attempts.

## User Stories
- As a user, when my search/query has a likely typo, I want a suggested corrected query so I can continue without retyping.
- As a user, when my prompt is ambiguous, I want concise alternative prompts so I can pick one that matches my intent.
- As a user, when an automated action would fail due to missing context, I want suggestions that add the required context.

## Acceptance Criteria
- Detect common misspellings, near-matches, and ambiguous queries with configurable thresholds.
- Present 1–3 suggestions inline beneath the input with clear labels (e.g., "Did you mean: …").
- Clicking a suggestion replaces the input or runs the corrected action, based on user preference.
- Accessibility: suggestions are reachable and operable via keyboard and screen readers.
- Analytics: log suggestion shown, accepted, and dismissed events.

## Design
- UI: Inline suggestion row beneath the input field; simple pill buttons for each suggestion plus a "Why this?" icon for context.
- Behavior: Non-blocking. Suggestions shown after user stops typing (debounced 300–500ms) or after a failed action.
- Config: Toggle in user preferences to auto-apply top suggestion on double-enter.

## Detection Rules (Implementation)
- Spellcheck & fuzzy-match against core domain vocabulary and entity names (Levenshtein distance or fuzzy search).
- Query intent clustering: map ambiguous prompts to high-confidence templates.
- Failure-triggered suggestions: if an action fails with known error types (missing field, unknown entity), generate suggestion to add/replace missing info.

## Data & Models
- Use lightweight local fuzzy-matching libraries for instant suggestions.
- Optionally call backend ML service for advanced intent rewriting (rate-limited and fallback to local).
- Keep suggestion generation deterministic and explainable; include sanitized example matches for debugging.

## API / Backend
- Endpoint: POST /api/suggestions with payload { input, context, locale } → returns [{ text, actionType, confidence, rationale }]
- Rate-limit and cache frequent queries.
- Log events: suggestion_shown, suggestion_accepted, suggestion_dismissed.

## Security & Privacy
- Do not send PII to external suggestion services without consent.
- Mask or strip sensitive fields before sending to any third-party model.
- Store suggestion logs with minimal PII and retention policy.

## Accessibility
- Ensure suggestion buttons are focusable (tab index) and have ARIA labels.
- Announce suggestions to screen readers when shown.
- Maintain sufficient color contrast for suggestion UI.

## Metrics
- Suggestion shown rate, acceptance rate, dismissal rate.
- Downstream success rate of accepted suggestions (task completion).
- Time-to-complete tasks with/without suggestions.

## Testing
- Unit tests for detection heuristics and threshold behavior.
- Integration tests for UI rendering and keyboard navigation.
- E2E tests simulating misspelled queries, ambiguous prompts, and failure-triggered flows.

## Rollout Plan
- Feature flag gated per env and per user (beta opt-in).
- Start with local fuzzy suggestions, monitor metrics, then enable backend ML option for opt-in users.
- Gradual percentage rollout with rollback threshold.

## Implementation Notes
- Keep UI lightweight and performant; debounce typing events.
- Provide developer-configurable vocab and synonyms file.
- Expose telemetry hooks for future A/B testing.

## Open Questions
- Default confidence threshold for showing suggestions?
- Which backend rewriting models (if any) to support and how to charge for usage?
- Policy for auto-applying suggestions vs manual acceptance?

## Acceptance Checklist
- [ ] Feature flag implemented
- [ ] UI component added with keyboard + screen reader support
- [ ] Suggestion backend endpoint available (or local fallback)
- [ ] Analytics events instrumented
- [ ] Privacy review completed

Small, focused feature that improves UX for ambiguous or error-prone inputs while preserving user control and privacy.